  "query_preview": "..Southeast Arizona is under mostly sunny skies,\nwith a few cumulus clouds along the International Border and\nafternoon breeziness. This is thanks to a shortwave trough that\nis moving through the area. \n\nThis weekend high pressure begins to build over the Rockies as a\nPacific low pressure system begins to dig down the West Coast.\nThese two systems will tighten the pressure gradient,\ntranslating into breezy to locally windy conditions for \nSoutheast Arizona, with the strongest winds expected on Sunday. \nSoutheast/east winds at 15-25 mph with gusts to 30-35 mph to \nthose locations prone to easterly wind, with the strongest winds\nwill be across the Gila River Valley, where southeast winds of \n20-30 mph with gusts to 35-40 mph may be possible. These winds \nmay result in areas of blowing dust (especially across the more \ndust-prone areas of the San Simon Valley which impact portions \nof Interstate-10 between Willcox and the AZ/NM state line). With\nthese winds, near critical fire weather conditions are possible\nin the Upper Gila River Valley and portions of Cochise and SE \nPinal counties, more in the fire weather section. This easterly \nflow will introduce just enough mid- level moisture into the \neastern portions of Southeast Arizona for a slight chance (10-15\npercent) of mainly dry thunderstorms Sunday and Monday.\n\nThe low pressure system will cut-off from the main flow as it\nbegins to dig down and takes it's time to work back mid next\nweek. This will keep our afternoon winds breezy each day. The\nsystem will then pass to the north of the area 'cooling' the\ntemperatures slightly Wednesday and Thursday.",
